#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192612 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5228a68-1e0a-5761-ac90-40fcab271214" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component from SVD"}] :question/id #uuid "b0a1a305-4617-50f6-a0d9-2b573277f68f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21883" :question/problem "![](images/29/296789d8eae5298a2e5710f7e7ad81f179a7bbcb1771ec483b3ff0ee3938bb82.svg)\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2 \\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X = U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the third principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\lambda_3\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1,$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2,$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue $\\lambda_3.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice the following:\n\n- The matrix $\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\Sigma^T \\Sigma$ is a diagonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\n- The matrix $V$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the third principal component is \n\n![](images/db/db03c4d83823d85b5397d300617361bd67cd69e013b29f7711c32a7ae711cc44.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192613 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dbfeaac-fc49-543d-b2b0-673b7dbb05d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance from SVD"}] :question/id #uuid "ad59f3c2-8ca8-5d05-b626-501682800f01" :question/math-academy-id "e-21884" :question/problem "A financial analyst measured $5$ features, $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_5,$ for each company among a group of companies under study. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular company.\n\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1=7, \\quad \\sigma_2=5, \\quad \\sigma_3=3, \\quad \\sigma_4=2, \\quad \\sigma_5=1.\n$$\n\nThe analyst then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y = X V_2,$ where $V_2$ is the $5 \\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY = XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features $y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_5$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features $y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_5$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values $\\sigma_1^2, \\sigma_2^2, \\ldots, \\sigma_5^2$ represent the variances in the respective features $y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_5.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2}+(5)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the analyst used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2}+(5)^{2} \\\\  & =74.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{74}{88} \\\\  & ≈0.841 \\\\  & =84.1%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5228a68-1e0a-5761-ac90-40fcab271214" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "338562de-dd6f-55a9-9685-cfa623899301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525334} {:answer/id #uuid "6409144c-b1a1-587a-a04a-d1cd257fbd6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525461} {:answer/id #uuid "9dff0d86-8ea4-5fbf-a597-15881b1bbaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525607} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c84af2-ad7a-5b36-935c-a3e3cd63600f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525680} {:answer/id #uuid "f406a27e-61d0-5a8c-82d6-d098c6b47b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8c84af2-ad7a-5b36-935c-a3e3cd63600f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525680} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e7a2b67-b371-5a62-947f-c82e0ac64156" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a712975-c967-5929-93fa-93c38ef8445c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343711" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2\\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the second principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the second principal component is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dbfeaac-fc49-543d-b2b0-673b7dbb05d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac7d70fa-bf88-5d8a-b589-211fc1508e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "86.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191824} {:answer/id #uuid "f22fa831-0ffa-5b44-8d4d-6444a734e999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191825} {:answer/id #uuid "229c730c-c45a-5588-82ae-49b26c273ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191826} {:answer/id #uuid "41964a93-85b9-57dd-b859-9b726749f309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191827} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4abaff-9204-5ea0-a799-46b24af32d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "229c730c-c45a-5588-82ae-49b26c273ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191826} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c783572-57af-5f59-b3ff-cca2d8bf3bb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edd41528-ab75-576f-8bc0-8fe5a9ab1f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-343713" :question/problem "A scientist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each specimen among a group of animals in danger of extinction. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular specimen.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=7,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.6,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.4.\n$$\n\nThe scientist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $5\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.6)^{2}+(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =54.52\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the scientist used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2} \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{49}{54.52} \\\\  & ≈0.899 \\\\  & =89.9%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5228a68-1e0a-5761-ac90-40fcab271214" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04efe72c-8db5-5234-84ae-3f4bdd2aafed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525217} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8aebf4-f940-5a38-a369-1714b61c658f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525396} {:answer/id #uuid "53817510-a718-5abb-8d2f-af58a7cad647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525422} {:answer/id #uuid "7083348b-36e4-559f-afc5-71cfd001fec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525491} {:answer/id #uuid "7635ff08-9f47-53ec-be22-adca432c8cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7083348b-36e4-559f-afc5-71cfd001fec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525491} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4fa04e8-a76f-5e24-bb73-629bec1ffe50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a56a93f9-03e9-5541-a7b2-0bb8dffd858c" :question/math-academy-id "q-345546" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2\\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the first principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the first principal component is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5228a68-1e0a-5761-ac90-40fcab271214" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08899ef8-2455-5e72-bd85-9a860c351a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525227} {:answer/id #uuid "8e586ceb-a35b-5388-988a-46403f83e804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525564} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9695cc-de94-5201-a833-0381b7a1cec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525687} {:answer/id #uuid "cef4e477-edb6-559c-ab8d-dee81343a7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525720} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc52cf4-b430-528d-bc91-af9a67c8ca92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfc52cf4-b430-528d-bc91-af9a67c8ca92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525722} :answer-field/id #uuid "94eae28f-0f0a-556f-8c19-4f2923019490" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94503ed0-de40-59e8-a833-79a2a877bd96" :question/math-academy-id "q-345556" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2\\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the third principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the third principal component is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5228a68-1e0a-5761-ac90-40fcab271214" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e9eaf29-8808-53c4-979e-04ff0d86ad30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525276} {:answer/id #uuid "311803db-3063-5d97-af68-dc746a6d1fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525325} {:answer/id #uuid "706acccb-d5be-5e23-bc39-eec9acea2725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525489} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fe5bed-815c-5c8c-ad44-010ec129bb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{1,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525744} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7496ee-90fa-52f4-90aa-f1d3a1d7a52e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{{u}^{T}}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "311803db-3063-5d97-af68-dc746a6d1fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525325} :answer-field/id #uuid "7594ca40-beac-52cc-95c3-8f0ed061fa22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7686a2d1-5498-5a0d-a8d1-3fd56d01887b" :question/math-academy-id "q-345566" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {u}_{1} & ⋯ & {u}_{n} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & ⋯ & {v}_{m} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $n\\times m$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the first principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $m\\times m$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $m\\times m$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the first principal component is ${v}_{1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5228a68-1e0a-5761-ac90-40fcab271214" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a737e15-e456-5ece-b7ba-2cb3a7ac9c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525259} {:answer/id #uuid "9074b51d-7c2f-5040-9b6b-b52e25c2436d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{4}{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525572} {:answer/id #uuid "a119d3ac-35b3-5b6c-ab81-7509eaf0009b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{4,4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525617} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d8bee9-429a-59d6-8620-da70cb2d9103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525668} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc761fe-dbd0-510b-9241-ecdf95f98556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{4}{{u}^{T}}_{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3d8bee9-429a-59d6-8620-da70cb2d9103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525668} :answer-field/id #uuid "e996ac02-f7f8-52a5-bf48-73bf10f4b3be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30651707-ffb3-536f-b51e-a5a6fc797509" :question/math-academy-id "q-345569" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {u}_{1} & ⋯ & {u}_{n} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & ⋯ & {v}_{m} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $n\\times m$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the fourth principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $m\\times m$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $m\\times m$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the fourth principal component is ${v}_{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dbfeaac-fc49-543d-b2b0-673b7dbb05d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c0350c-0471-55de-ac70-f2529da87d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525251} {:answer/id #uuid "1bed26d7-5712-5fad-9a47-4f08bb3eb187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525265} {:answer/id #uuid "33b6e47c-35b6-5317-94f8-1bf5ec2d2141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525335} {:answer/id #uuid "544af06f-53bf-5a45-9d45-b81c4c92cd56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525425} {:answer/id #uuid "9042a2f9-0756-5b9d-ad5e-9f3310959eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16c0350c-0471-55de-ac70-f2529da87d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525251} :answer-field/id #uuid "04e2e485-a811-5a11-9f6d-621be11e5c98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b0e0b9e-9780-58a6-9804-62e273336ea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-345598" :question/problem "A water pollution researcher measured $4$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},$ for each water sample in a region with suspicions of water pollution. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular sample.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=9,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe researcher then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $4\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(9)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  researcher used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(9)^{2} \\\\  & =81.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{81}{95} \\\\  & ≈0.853 \\\\  & =85.3%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dbfeaac-fc49-543d-b2b0-673b7dbb05d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01d7962d-301d-5564-b84c-5c4c275c4f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525214} {:answer/id #uuid "2101ed94-0565-58d9-96d4-55fa409f7603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525280} {:answer/id #uuid "62e6b56c-6deb-528d-b4f2-3731cf383635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525455} {:answer/id #uuid "d844e003-3bf2-5bc1-b18b-41cf1342c36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96.0%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525743} {:answer/id #uuid "f856d479-2998-5675-8c7e-c2b51e51ddc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2101ed94-0565-58d9-96d4-55fa409f7603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525280} :answer-field/id #uuid "2547a1a4-4d8c-5e2b-a028-871e88aaff4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "544f3b17-0710-5a62-83f3-a61ed0f7bcdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-345600" :question/problem "A quality inspector measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each machine in a factory. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular machine.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=6,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=4,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.5.\n$$\n\nThe inspector then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{2},$ where ${V}_{2}$ is the $5\\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =57.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the inspector used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(4)^{2} \\\\  & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{52}{57.25} \\\\  & ≈0.908 \\\\  & =90.8%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dbfeaac-fc49-543d-b2b0-673b7dbb05d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a97efd-6507-568d-a733-df35803cdd38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525279} {:answer/id #uuid "37741afd-0543-580c-8a1e-3d6da126b644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "86.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525342} {:answer/id #uuid "b05f07be-33d9-5fa6-b04b-06111535bbdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525653} {:answer/id #uuid "da8a6c89-592b-5d39-adbd-d1d8791272ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525747} {:answer/id #uuid "dab33267-108b-5e93-b7c9-075b6c4a7248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da8a6c89-592b-5d39-adbd-d1d8791272ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525747} :answer-field/id #uuid "337c107f-d7b5-5336-9dff-a1373b9e6812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7dcc2367-ae8e-5075-82b4-bcd5d8410a2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-345605" :question/problem "A nutritionist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each client in a group under a specific diet. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular client.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=6,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.6.\n$$\n\nThe nutritionist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{2},$ where ${V}_{2}$ is the $5\\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.6)^{2} \\\\  & =50.36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the nutritionist used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(3)^{2} \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{45}{50.36} \\\\  & ≈0.894 \\\\  & =89.4%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dbfeaac-fc49-543d-b2b0-673b7dbb05d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516d873e-ccd4-5077-a4ce-d8e999a1ad90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525417} {:answer/id #uuid "7373c834-8f8c-55c5-9e2e-b8721d241460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525499} {:answer/id #uuid "8d89f02b-374b-52f6-a7e9-61fe7c34455e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525559} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc471c5-7d2f-5671-88e6-6184f6fbad95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 175921